Wealth Distribution.-We use wealth distribution data from the Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF) 2007. ${ }^{17}$ The wealth variable we use is net wealth, the sum of net financial wealth and housing, minus any debts. The distribution is very skewed to the right. We take the shares from the cleaned version in Díaz-Giménez, Glover, and Ríos-Rull (2011). Figure 2 displays the histogram of the wealth distribution.

Table 2 displays the wealth share moments we use.
Social Mobility.-As for wealth transition across generations, we use the mobility matrix calculated by Charles and Hurst (2003), Table 2, from PSID data. This matrix is constructed by means of pairs of simultaneously alive parent and child of different ages. To eliminate age effects, the matrix is obtained by computing transitions from the residuals of the wealth of parents and children after conditioning on age and age squared.

